<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-techniques-limits">
    <title>1.2 Techniques for Finding Limits</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-limit-principles">
 <title>Limit Principles</title>
 <statement>
     <p>
  If <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}f(x)=L</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}g(x)=M</m>, then:
  <ol marker="1">
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}c=c</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}[f(x)]^n = L^n</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}\sqrt[n]{f(x)} = \sqrt[n]{L}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><term>Sum/Difference:</term> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}[f(x)\pm g(x)] = L \pm M</m></p></li>
      <li><p><term>Product:</term> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}[f(x)\cdot g(x)] = L \cdot M</m></p></li>
      <li><p><term>Quotient:</term> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}\frac{g(x)}{f(x)} = \frac{M}{L}</m>, if <m>L \neq 0</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}cf(x) = c \cdot L</m></p></li>
      <li><p>If <m>f</m> is a polynomial, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}f(x)=f(a)</m></p></li>
      <li><p>If <m>r</m> is a rational function, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}r(x)=r(a)</m> (where defined)</p></li>
  </ol>
     </p>
 </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
    We can therefore use ordinary substitution and algebraic simplification, 
    such as canceling out common factors in the numerator and denominator of 
    a rational function, to help us determine limits. 
</p>

    <example xml:id="example-substitution">
 <title>Limit by Direct Substitution</title>
 <statement>
     <p>Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{x^3+1}{2x}</m>.</p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
     <p>
  By direct substitution: to calculate this limit we "plug in" 2 in place of <m>x</m> to get: 
  <me>\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{x^3+1}{2x} = \frac{2^3+1}{2(2)} = \frac{9}{4}</me>.
     </p>
 </solution>
    </example>

<example xml:id="example-factoring-negative">
 <title>Limit by Factoring</title>
 <statement>
     <p>Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\frac{x+3}{x^2-9}</m>.</p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
     <p>
        Here, when we "plug in" -3 we get <m>0/0</m>. 
        We factor the denominator: this means that -3 is a root of both the numerator and 
        the denominator. Hence we can factor out <m>x-(-3) = x+3</m>
         from both the numerator and the denominator to get: 
  
  <me>\lim_{x\to -3}\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-3)} = \lim_{x\to -3}\frac{1}{x-3} = \frac{1}{-3-3} = -\frac{1}{6}</me>.
     </p>
 </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-conjugate-simple">
 <title>Limit involving Square Roots</title>
 <statement>
     <p>If <m>f(x)= \frac{x-9}{\sqrt{x}-3}</m>, find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 9} f(x)</m>.</p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
     <p>
  Factoring the numerator as a difference of squares:
  <me>\lim_{x \to 9} \frac{(\sqrt{x}-3)(\sqrt{x}+3)}{\sqrt{x}-3} = \lim_{x \to 9} (\sqrt{x}+3) = \sqrt{9}+3 = 6</me>.
     </p>
 </solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="ex-various-techniques">
 <title>Mixed Limit Practice</title>
 <statement>
     <p>
  Find the following limits:
  <ol marker="a">
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2}(x^4-5x^3+x^2-7)</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}\sqrt{x^2-3x+2}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -3}\frac{x^2-9}{x+3}</m></p></li>
  </ol>
     </p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
     <p>
  <ol marker="a">
      <li><p><m>2^4 - 5(2^3) + 2^2 - 7 = 16 - 40 + 4 - 7 = -27</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\sqrt{0^2 - 3(0) + 2} = \sqrt{2}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>\lim_{x \to -3} \frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x+3} = \lim_{x \to -3} (x-3) = -6</m></p></li>
  </ol>
     </p>
 </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-piecewise">
 <title>Limit of a Piecewise Function</title>
 <statement>
     <p>
  Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1} g(x)</m> where 
  <me>g(x)=\begin{cases} x+1 \amp \text{if } x \neq 1 \\ \pi \amp \text{if } x=1 \end{cases}</me>
     </p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
  <figure xml:id="fig-limit-g">
  <caption>The graph of <m> g(x)</m> showing the limit as <m>x \to 1</m>.</caption>
  <image width="60%">
    <description>A line with slope 1 and y-intercept 1. There is an open circle at (1,2) and a solid dot at (1, pi).</description>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
            axis lines = middle,
            xlabel = \(x\),
            ylabel = \(y\),
            xmin = -0.5, xmax = 2.5,
            ymin = 0, ymax = 4,
            xtick = {1},
            ytick = {1, 2, 3.14159},
            yticklabels = {1, 2, \(\pi\)},
          ]
          <!-- The linear pieces -->
          \addplot[domain=-0.5:0.92, thick, blue] {x+1};
          \addplot[domain=1.08:2.5, thick, blue] {x+1};
          
          <!-- The removable discontinuity (hole) -->
          \draw[thick, blue] (axis cs:1,2) circle [radius=2pt];
          
          <!-- The actual function value at x=1 -->
          \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(1, 3.14159)};
          
          <!-- Optional dashed lines to show limit -->
          \draw[dashed] (axis cs:1,0) -- (axis cs:1,2);
          \draw[dashed] (axis cs:0,2) -- (axis cs:1,2);
        \end{axis}
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
</figure>

     <p>
  The limit depends only on values near 1, not at <m>x=1</m>.
  As <m>x \to 1</m>, <m>g(x) = x+1</m>. Thus, the limit is <m>1+1=2</m>.
     </p>
 </solution>
</exercise>

<exercises xml:id="exercises-limits-algebraic">
    <title>Limits Practice</title>

    <!-- Problem 1 -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}\frac{4-\sqrt{16+h}}{h}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Direct substitution results in the indeterminate form <m>0/0</m>. 
                We rationalize the numerator by multiplying by the conjugate:
                <me>
                    \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{4-\sqrt{16+h}}{h} \cdot \frac{4+\sqrt{16+h}}{4+\sqrt{16+h}} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{16-(16+h)}{h(4+\sqrt{16+h})}
                </me>
                Simplifying the numerator gives <m>-h</m>, which allows us to cancel the <m>h</m> in the denominator:
                <me>
                    \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-h}{h(4+\sqrt{16+h})} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-1}{4+\sqrt{16+h}} = -\frac{1}{8}
                </me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Problem 2 -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow 5^+}(\sqrt{x^2-25}+3)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Since we are approaching 5 from the right (<m>x > 5</m>), the term <m>x^2 - 25</m> is a small positive number. 
                We can evaluate this by direct substitution:
                <me>\sqrt{5^2-25}+3 = \sqrt{0}+3 = 3</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Problem 3 -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}\left(\frac{1}{h}\right)\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+h}}-1\right)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, find a common denominator for the second term:
                <me>\frac{1}{h} \left( \frac{1 - \sqrt{1+h}}{\sqrt{1+h}} \right)</me>
                Now multiply by the conjugate <m>1 + \sqrt{1+h}</m>:
                <me>
                    \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{1-(1+h)}{h\sqrt{1+h}(1+\sqrt{1+h})} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-h}{h\sqrt{1+h}(1+\sqrt{1+h})}
                </me>
                Canceling <m>h</m> and substituting <m>h=0</m> gives <m>\frac{-1}{1(1+1)} = -1/2</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Problem 4 -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow 1}\left(\frac{x^2}{x-1}-\frac{1}{x-1}\right)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Combine the fractions over the common denominator:
                <me>\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{x^2-1}{x-1}</me>
                Factor the difference of squares:
                <me>\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1} = \lim_{x \to 1} (x+1) = 2</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Problem 5 -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow 1} \frac{x-1}{\sqrt{x+3}-2}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, <m>\sqrt{x+3}+2</m>:
                <me>
                    \lim_{x \to 1} \frac{(x-1)(\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{(x+3)-4} = \lim_{x \to 1} \frac{(x-1)(\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x-1}
                </me>
                Canceling <m>x-1</m> leaves <m>\sqrt{1+3}+2 = 4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</exercises>




<theorem xml:id="thm-sandwich">
 <title>Sandwich Theorem (Squeeze Theorem)</title>
 <statement>
     <p>
  Suppose <m>f(x) \leq h(x) \leq g(x)</m> near <m>a</m> (except possibly at <m>a</m>).
  If <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}f(x)=L=\lim_{x \to a}g(x)</m>, then <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}h(x)=L</m>.
     </p>
 </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="example-sandwich-sine">
 <title>Proving a Limit with Sandwich Theorem</title>
 <statement>
     <p>Prove <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0}x^2\sin\left(\frac{1}{x^2}\right)=0</m>.</p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
     <p>
  Since <m>-1 \leq \sin(\theta) \leq 1</m>, we have:
  <me>-1 \leq \sin\left(\frac{1}{x^2}\right) \leq 1</me>
  Multiply by <m>x^2</m> (which is always <m>\geq 0</m>):
  <me>-x^2 \leq x^2\sin\left(\frac{1}{x^2}\right) \leq x^2</me>
  Since <m>\lim_{x \to 0}(-x^2) = 0</m> and <m>\lim_{x \to 0}(x^2) = 0</m>, by the Sandwich Theorem, the limit is 0.
     </p>
 </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0} |x|</m> by using the Sandwich Theorem.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            For any real number <m>x</m>, we know the absolute value is bounded by:
            <me>-|x| \le x \le |x|</me>
            To squeeze <m>|x|</m> specifically, we use the fact that:
            <me>0 \le |x| \le |x|</me>
            More formally, observe that for all <m>x</m> in the interval <m>(-1, 1)</m>:
            <me>0 \le |x| \le \sqrt{x^2}</me>
            Since <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} 0 = 0</m> and <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \sqrt{x^2} = 0</m>, by the Sandwich Theorem:
            <me>\lim_{x \to 0} |x| = 0</me>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

</section>
